body.en:
A newspaper editor in Kenya was kidnapped from a car late on Tuesday and then located near a dam far away a few hours later. The Standard paper said it suspected state involvement in an attempt to intimidate reporters.

An associate editor for one of Kenya 's largest news outlets, The Standard , was abducted by armed men overnight and then released near a dam far from the scene around nine hours later, his employer said on Wednesday.&#160;

Alex Kiprotich was taken from a car at around 9:20 p.m. on Tuesday on a road near the city of Nakuru.

"The circumstances of Mr&#160;Kiprotich's abduction remain deeply troubling, and we will continue to press for full accountability," the government-critical outlet said in a press release after his discovery.&#160;

Late on Tuesday the Standard Group's CEO Chaacha Mwita had also said that the "abduction bears all the hallmarks of a targeted attack on a journalist for doing his job."

The newspaper released a video overnight of what appeared to be dashcam footage from the car Kiprotich was traveling in when he was abducted.

A white vehicle without any markings apparent in the&#160;footage&#160;came to a stop in front of the car in an area where temporary barriers created a single-lane bottleneck on&#160;the Gilgil-Nakuru highway, and put on its hazard lights.

Four men in civilian clothes and obscuring their faces —&#160;some holding firearms and others carrying what appeared to be baseball bats — emerged and converged on the car, soon taking a man back to their vehicle and driving off.&#160;

On Wednesday, Standard Group said that Kiprotich had been located near a dam more than&#160;200 kilometers (120 miles) southeast the scene of the abduction roughly nine hours earlier.&#160;

"Mr Kiprotich was located by a Standard Group team led by the Group CEO Chaacha Mwita at around 6:30 a.m. near Masinga Dam, after being dumped by his captors in the early hours of the morning," it said.&#160;

Kiprotic was "safe, though understandably shaken by his ordeal," his employer said. Kiprotich works for newspaper The Standard , a flagship of the wider media group known for its critical coverage of President William Ruto's government .&#160;

Police and the government in Kenya did not immediately comment on the incident.&#160;

Dozens of government critics and activists have been abducted in Kenya this year alone, according to a report released this week, with the practice becoming more common since widespread anti-government protests in 2024.&#160;

Human rights groups have accused state agencies of being responsible, with Standard Group voicing similar suspicions in this case.&#160;

Kiprotich had said in June that he narrowly escaped a previous attempt to kidnap him, which came three days after Ruto called out the Standard...
